// Expected: a0 at one session and at two.
//
// Challenge-response with the responder's signature bound to the verifier's
// own nonce. This is the shape that is supposed to survive replication, and
// the model exists to show that it does for a reason rather than by accident.
//
// The attacker has two cross-session moves and neither pays. Handing Bob the
// other session's signature fails SIGNVERIF, because that signature covers
// nb#2 and this Bob is checking against nb, so Bob halts. Relaying nb into
// the second session first -- nb is unguarded on its way to Alice#2 -- does
// make Alice#2 sign nb, but a signature over nb under the same long-term key
// is the identical term to the one Alice already produced, so delivering it
// is a replay of a value indistinguishable from the honest one.
//
// Both routes are therefore non-attacks for different reasons: the first
// halts, the second reduces to the honest value. Contrast
// session_psk_cross_feed.vp, where the responder contributes nothing and the
// same relay succeeds.

attacker[active]

principal Bob[
	generates nb
]

Bob -> Alice: nb

principal Alice[
	knows private sk
	pk = PUBKEY(sk)
	sg = SIGN(sk, nb)
]

Alice -> Bob: [pk], sg

principal Bob[
	_ = SIGNVERIF(pk, nb, sg)?
	accepted_b = HASH(nb, sg)
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: sg
]
